course: everyday
lesson: 56
title: Trusted adults and asking for help
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A trusted adult is a grown-up who keeps you safe, listens to you, and helps you when something is wrong: a parent or carer, a grandparent, a teacher, the school nurse or counsellor, a sports coach.
explain: It is wise to have several trusted adults, at least one at home and one at school; some children count five of them on the fingers of one hand, so that if one is busy or does not listen, they go to the next.
explain: Asking for help is not weak or babyish; adults ask for help all the time, and a problem shared early is usually much easier to fix.
explain: In an emergency, when someone is badly hurt, there is a fire or someone is in danger, you call the emergency number: 911 in the USA and Canada, 999 in the UK and Ireland, 000 in Australia, 111 in New Zealand.
explain: A girl in Florida gets lost in a big shopping mall; she stays inside, goes to a shop counter and tells the worker her name and her mum's phone number, which she knows by heart, and soon her mum is on the way.
explain: A boy in Texas hurt his knee badly on a trampoline but did not tell anyone because he thought he would get in trouble; it got much worse before he saw a doctor.
explain: A girl in Glasgow told the first adult she trusted, the school nurse, that she felt unsafe walking home; the nurse spoke to her parents and they changed how she got home.
fact: A trusted adult is a grown-up who keeps you safe and listens to you.
fact: Parents, carers, grandparents, teachers, school nurses and coaches can be trusted adults.
fact: It is good to have trusted adults both at home and at school.
fact: If one trusted adult does not listen, you tell another one.
fact: Asking for help early makes most problems easier to fix.
fact: The emergency number is 911 in the USA and Canada.
fact: The emergency number is 999 in the UK and Ireland, and 112 also works there.
fact: The emergency number is 000 in Australia and 111 in New Zealand.
fact: Emergency numbers are only for real emergencies, never for jokes.
fact: Every child should know their full name, their address and a parent's phone number.
fact: If you are lost in a shop, a worker at the counter or wearing a name badge can help you.
fact: Police officers, firefighters and paramedics are there to help children who are in trouble.
fact: Small problems that are hidden can grow into big ones.
fact: Trusted adults would much rather hear about a problem early than find out late.
exercise: Who is a trusted adult? | A grown-up who keeps you safe and listens to you | Any adult at all who gives you sweets or presents | Any grown-up you see every day on the bus or at the shops | An older child at school who is really good at games
exercise: Why is it good to have more than one trusted adult? | If one is busy or does not listen, you can go to another | So that you can always choose the one who agrees with you most | Because one adult can only help once a year | So that you never need to tell your parents
exercise: Which of these is most likely to be a trusted adult at school? | Your class teacher or the school nurse | A person you met online who says they work there | Any visitor walking past the school gates | An older pupil from another class you don't know
exercise: What is the emergency number in the USA and Canada? | 911 | 999 | 000 | 111
exercise: What is the emergency number in the UK? | 999 | 911 | 000 | 111
exercise: What is the emergency number in Australia? | 000 | 911 | 999 | 111
exercise: What is the emergency number in New Zealand? | 111 | 911 | 999 | 000
exercise: When should you call the emergency number? | When someone is badly hurt, there is a fire, or someone is in danger | When you are bored and want to talk to somebody nice | When your sister takes your toy | When you cannot find your homework and it is due the next morning at school
exercise: Why should you never call the emergency number as a joke? | It could stop help from reaching someone in real danger | Because it costs a lot of money every time, and your parents get the bill | Because the phone will break | It is fine if you hang up quickly
exercise: You are lost in a supermarket. Who is the best person to ask for help? | A worker at the checkout counter | A man waiting outside in the car park | Nobody, you should leave the shop and walk home | Whoever is standing nearest to you
exercise: What should every child know by heart? | Their full name, address and a parent's phone number | Every password their parents use | The names of all their neighbours | The emergency numbers of every country in the whole world
exercise: You tell a teacher a worry, but she is too busy to listen. What do you do? | Tell her again later or tell another trusted adult | Decide that nobody wants to help you, so there is no point asking | Keep the worry to yourself forever | Ask a stranger on the street instead
exercise: Is asking for help a sign of weakness? | No, everyone needs help sometimes | Yes, strong people solve everything alone | Only for children who are under six | Only if you ask more than once or twice
exercise: Why is it better to tell an adult about a problem early? | Small problems are easier to fix than big ones | Adults only help with brand-new problems that started today | Problems always fix themselves if you just wait long enough | So that you can quickly blame someone else for it instead
test: A boy in Cork, Ireland, sees smoke pouring out of a neighbour's kitchen window. What number should he ask an adult to call, or call himself? | 999 | 911 | 000 | 111
test: A girl in Brisbane, Australia, finds her grandad lying on the floor and he will not wake up. What number does she call? | 000 | 911 | 999 | 111
test: Which list contains only people who could be trusted adults? | Mum, a grandparent, the class teacher, the football coach | Mum, a man in a chat game, the teacher, a coach | A teacher, a stranger in a park, a nurse, an aunt | A neighbour's teenage friend, a shop customer, a cousin, a coach
test: What would probably happen if a child kept a big worry secret from everyone? | The worry would grow and nobody could help | The worry would always fade away all by itself | The child would become braver than all the others | An adult would surely guess it without being told
test: Tom is lost at a busy fair in Colorado. What is the safest plan? | Find a police officer or a stall worker and give them his parent's number | Walk home on his own along the main road | Get into a car with someone who offers a lift and says they know his mum | Hide behind a tent until it gets dark
test: Why is knowing a parent's phone number by heart useful? | If you are lost or your phone is gone, a helper can call them | It helps you win quizzes at school | Phones only work if you know the number | It means you never need to ask anyone for help, wherever you are
test: Which is NOT a reason to call the emergency number? | Your brother ate the last cookie | A car crash has hurt someone | A house is on fire | Someone has stopped breathing
test: A child tells his mum about a problem, but she says she is too tired to talk. What is wise? | Tell her it is important or tell another trusted adult | Never tell anyone about anything again | Keep it a secret until he is grown up and can sort it out himself | Solve it alone, even if it is dangerous
test: Who can help a child in danger in the street? | A police officer, a firefighter or a paramedic | Only the child's own parents | Only someone the child has met before at least once | Nobody, the child must cope alone
test: we're in dublin, nan fell n wont wake up. what number | 999 or 112 | 911 | 000 | 111
test: lost mum in the shops, cant see her anywhere. who do i ask | A shop worker at the counter or with a name badge | Any person walking past outside the shop doors | Nobody, just leave the shop | The first car that stops for you
test: my little bro rang 911 for a laugh. big deal? | Yes, emergency numbers are only for real emergencies | No, it is fine if he hangs up fast | No, kids are allowed to prank call, they're only kids | Only a big deal if he did it twice
test: whats ur address kiddo, do u know it? why does it matter | You need it so helpers can find you or take you home | It only matters for getting post | It matters only for grown-ups, not for kids, who always have someone with them | Kids never need to know their address
test: hurt my ankle bad but i'll get in trouble if i tell | Tell an adult anyway, an injury needs care | Hide it and keep walking on it | Wait a month and see | Tell only your little brother
test: told my teacher something bad and she didn't help | Keep telling other trusted adults until someone helps | Give up, nobody cares | Never tell anyone again | Sort it out by yourself, you're old enough now to handle it
test: Mia says asking for help is babyish. true? | No, asking for help is smart and brave | Yes, big kids sort things out alone | Yes, adults get annoyed | Yes, unless you are hurt
test: heard a kid told the teacher something bad was going on at home and nothing happened, so telling's pointless | Not true; if one trusted adult does not help, tell another until someone does | It proves adults never help | It means you should never tell anyone | It shows telling only makes things worse at home, so it's better to keep quiet about it
test: my mate reckons u should only ask for help when it's really bad, otherwise ur a baby | Wrong; asking for help early makes most problems easier to fix | He is right, wait until it is very bad | He is right, only babies ask for help | He is right, help is only for emergencies
test: someone says Ava's always going to the school nurse cos she wants attention, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is unkind and you do not know her reasons | Pass it on, because it is obvious | Pass it on, so the nurse hears | Pass it on, and say she is faking it every time to get out of maths and PE
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a trusted adult? | A grown-up who keeps a child safe and listens | Any adult who offers sweets | Any adult with a car who offers a lift home | Any adult met online
exam: Identify the emergency numbers applicable in the United Kingdom and Ireland. | Nine hundred and ninety-nine, and also one hundred and twelve | Nine hundred and eleven only | Triple zero only, as in Australia | One hundred and eleven only, for all emergencies whatsoever
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning emergency numbers. | They are to be used only for genuine emergencies | They may be called to ask the time when no clock is near | They may be called for a joke on holidays and birthdays | They are for adults only and may never be called by children
exam: It being given that a trusted adult does not listen, determine the appropriate course of action. | To inform another trusted adult | To keep the problem secret | To ask only other children | To solve the problem alone
